course: technology
lesson: 24
title: Ships, ports and submarines
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A steel nail dropped in the bath sinks at once, yet a steel ship as long as three football pitches floats. The secret is shape: the hollow hull pushes aside a huge amount of water, and the water pushes back up with a force as big as the weight of the water pushed away.
explain: If the ship pushes aside water that weighs as much as the whole ship before its deck reaches the waterline, it floats. A squashed lump of the same steel pushes aside very little water, so it sinks.
explain: Squash a ball of modelling clay and it sinks in a bowl of water; press the same clay into a little boat and it floats. Ships are clay boats made of steel.
explain: Around the ships there is a whole world of machines: cranes stacking containers, lighthouses flashing their own patterns, locks lifting boats up hills of water, and submarines that sink and rise on purpose.
fact: A ship floats because its hollow shape pushes aside water weighing as much as the ship; a steel nail sinks but a steel ship floats.
fact: Never play near harbour edges, quays or mooring ropes; the water is deep and cold and ropes can snap tight.
fact: Container ships carry thousands of metal boxes of standard sizes, so any crane, ship, train or lorry can lift and carry them; most things in our shops travelled part of the way in a container.
fact: Cars drive onto a ferry's big decks, often through doors in the bow that lift up like a mouth; passengers keep to the passenger decks and listen to safety announcements.
fact: A submarine fills its tanks with water to sink and blows the water out with air to rise; its strong walls resist the squeeze of deep water, and some submarines stay under for months.
fact: Big nuclear submarines make their own oxygen from seawater using electricity.
fact: A lifejacket's foam or gas-filled bag keeps your head above water; wear one on boats, and if you fall into cold water, float on your back, keep calm and wave or shout for help.
fact: In the UK you call 999 and ask for the coastguard; the RNLI lifeboat crews are mostly volunteers.
fact: Each lighthouse flashes its own pattern, so sailors know which one they are seeing; British lighthouses now run automatically, without keepers.
fact: An anchor is a heavy shaped hook that digs into the seabed, and the weight of its chain helps hold the ship; a giant ship's anchor can weigh more than ten cars.
fact: A canal lock lifts or lowers boats by filling or emptying a chamber between gates; the Panama Canal's locks lift ships about 26 metres. Locks are deep, so stay back from the edge.
fact: A propeller's twisted blades push water backwards as they spin, so the ship moves forward; the biggest ship propellers are taller than a house.
fact: Sonar sends sound pulses down and listens for echoes from the seabed or fish; dolphins and bats use echoes in a similar way.
exercise: How can a heavy steel ship float when a steel nail sinks? | Its hollow shape pushes aside water weighing as much as the ship | Steel becomes lighter once it touches sea water | Ships are full of a gas that is lighter than air | Ships are held up by the waves pushing them
exercise: Why are shipping containers made in standard sizes? | So any crane, ship, train or lorry can lift and carry them | So that the boxes look tidy in photos | Because it is the only size of steel sheet that factories have ever made | So that pirates cannot tell them apart
exercise: How do cars get onto a big ferry? | They drive on, often through doors in the bow that lift up | A crane lifts each car on one by one | They are rolled on by passengers pushing them up a long ramp from the beach | They float alongside and are pulled on board
exercise: How does a submarine sink on purpose? | It fills its tanks with water to make itself heavier | It switches off its lights so it gets heavier | It folds its wings and dives like a bird | It drops a heavy anchor that pulls it down
exercise: How does a submarine rise back up? | It blows the water out of its tanks with air | It throws out its food and fuel | It waits for the tide to lift it | It spins its propeller upwards like a helicopter
exercise: Where do big nuclear submarines get oxygen to breathe for months? | They make it from seawater using electricity | They come up for air every hour | They carry it all in balloons | They breathe through a long pipe to the surface
exercise: What does a lifejacket do? | Its foam or gas bag keeps your head above water | It warms up the cold water all around your whole body | It lets you breathe underwater through a tiny tube | It makes you swim much faster, like a pair of flippers
exercise: You fall into cold water near the shore. What is the best first thing to do? | Float on your back, keep calm and wave or shout for help | Swim as hard as you can straight out to sea, away from the rocks | Take off all your clothes to swim faster | Dive down to where the water is warmer
exercise: How do sailors know which lighthouse they are seeing at night? | Each lighthouse flashes its own pattern | Each lighthouse has a name painted in lights | All lighthouses are the same, so they guess | The lighthouse keeper shouts its name aloud
exercise: Why doesn't a ship drift away when it stops for the night? | Its anchor digs into the seabed, helped by the heavy chain | It switches its engines off, and the still water holds it in one place | Sea water is thick and stops ships drifting | The lighthouse keeps an eye on it
exercise: How can a boat on a canal go uphill? | A lock fills with water and lifts the boat to the next level | The boat is pulled up a slope by horses | The canal tilts like a seesaw | The boat's engine drives it up a waterfall
exercise: About how high do the Panama Canal's locks lift a ship? | About 26 metres | Only about 2 metres, the height of a door | About 260 metres, as high as a tall tower | About 2,600 metres, higher than any mountain in Britain
exercise: Why are a ship's propeller blades twisted? | So as they spin they push water backwards and the ship forward | So they look nice when the ship is in port | So that fish cannot swim into them | So they can cut through seaweed
exercise: How does a ship's sonar find the seabed? | It sends sound pulses down and listens for the echoes | It shines a very bright light to the bottom and films what it sees | It drops a camera on a rope every minute | It feels the bottom with a long metal pole
exercise: Which animals find their way by echoes, a bit like sonar? | Dolphins and bats | Cows and horses in fields | Goldfish and garden snails | Pigeons and barn owls
exercise: A container ship has 5 rows of stacks; each row has 4 stacks of 6 containers. How many containers? | 120 | 15 | 24 | 100
test: «a steel ship floats coz steel floats in salt water, simple» What is right? | No, a steel nail sinks; the ship floats because of its hollow shape | Yes, all steel floats in salty water | Yes, but only in very cold water | No, ships float because their engines keep pushing them up out of the water
test: Lily squashes her clay boat into a ball and puts it back in the bowl. What happens? | It sinks, because a lump pushes aside too little water | It floats higher, because it is rounder | It floats the same, because it is the same clay and the same weight as before | It spins round in the water
test: «let's play tag on the harbour wall, way more fun than the beach» What is the answer? | No, harbour edges are deep, cold water with ropes and boats | Yes, harbours are safe because the water is calm and the walls are wide | Yes, if you stay away from the lighthouse | Only at high tide
test: What would happen if a submarine's tanks could not be emptied of water? | It could not rise back to the surface | It would rise to the top much faster than usual | It would float up and lie on its side in the water | Nothing, it would just switch on its lights
test: «ugh, why do all the containers look the same, so boring» What is the smart answer? | So any crane, ship, train or lorry in the world can handle them | Because nobody had paint of other colours | Because they all belong to one shop | So that they all float together in a neat line when they get dropped in the sea
test: In a pirate film, the captain steers by the lighthouse, but every lighthouse along the coast flashes the same. What is wrong? | Real lighthouses each flash their own pattern so sailors know which is which | Nothing, all lighthouses look the same | Lighthouses don't flash, they glow steadily | Pirates never used lighthouses
test: «the bridge? nah, we just climb on the lock gates to cross, it's faster» What is right? | Stay back: locks are deep, the water is cold and gates move | Fine, lock gates are made for walking on, they have handrails all the way | Fine, if no boat is in the lock | Only dangerous for little kids
test: «that anchor's just a big hook, weighs like a bike, right» How heavy can a giant ship's anchor be? | Heavier than ten cars | About as heavy as one bike | About as heavy as one family car | About as heavy as a school bag
test: «mate reckons lighthouses still have lonely keepers living in them» What is true for British lighthouses? | They now run automatically, without keepers | Every one has a keeper and a cat living in it | They are only switched on at Christmas time | They are all run by the RNLI's volunteers
test: A fishing boat's screen shows a big blob halfway between the boat and the seabed. How did it see it? | Sonar sent a sound pulse and heard an echo from a shoal of fish | A diver swam down and took a photo | A satellite looked down through the water from space and spotted the fish | The boat's anchor touched the fish
test: Count them: a ferry carries 30 cars and 4 lorries. How many vehicles? | 34 | 26 | 30 | 120
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a vessel constructed of steel remains afloat, notwithstanding that a steel nail sinks. | Its hollow form displaces a volume of water the weight of which equals that of the vessel | Steel is rendered lighter upon immersion in sea water | The vessel is filled with a gas lighter than air | The vessel is supported continually by the motion of the waves
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the means whereby a submarine submerges and surfaces. | It admits water to its tanks in order to descend and expels that water with compressed air in order to ascend | It descends by extinguishing its lights and ascends by illuminating them | It descends exclusively by means of an anchor lowered to the seabed | It is raised and lowered by the action of the tides alone
exam: Ascertain the course of action recommended to a person who has fallen into cold water. | To float upon the back, to remain calm and to signal for assistance | To swim with maximum exertion directly away from the shore | To remove all garments in order to swim with greater speed and less hindrance | To dive to a greater depth in search of warmer water
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the operation of a canal lock? | A chamber enclosed by gates is filled or emptied so as to raise or lower a vessel between levels | The canal is tilted mechanically so that vessels may roll uphill | Vessels are hauled up an inclined bank by draught horses | The vessel's engine propels it up a waterfall
